In 1904, a British mathematician named Gilbert Walker became head of the weather service in India. A failed [[monsoon|monsoon]] in 1899 had led to a terrible [[famine|famine]], and he wanted to forecast the rains. Walker and a team of assistants, doing calculations by hand, compared weather records from all over the world.

In the 1920s, he found a giant seesaw in air pressure: when it was high over the Pacific, it tended to be low over the Indian Ocean, and the other way round. He called it the Southern Oscillation. Decades later, scientists realised it was the air half of El Niño. That is why the cycle carries his word "oscillation" in its name.

{{story:ocean-currents}}

In the 1990s, NASA's Galileo spacecraft, named after the [[astronomer|astronomer]], flew close to Europa many times. It found that the ice is marked by long ridges and patches called chaos terrain. There, blocks of ice seem to have broken, drifted and frozen again, like sea ice breaking up in the Arctic.

The surface has very few [[crater|craters]]. That means it is young, perhaps tens of millions of years old, so something keeps renewing it. Galileo also measured how Europa responds to Jupiter's powerful magnetic field. The results fit best with a layer of salty, electrically conducting water beneath the ice.

{{story:deep-sea-vents}}

Earthquakes don't always make tsunamis. To be sure, scientists use deep-sea sensors called DART buoys, developed by NOAA in the USA. A pressure sensor sits on the ocean floor, sometimes 6,000 m down. When a tsunami passes over, the extra water above it pushes a little harder.

The sensor sends a sound signal up to a buoy floating on the surface. The buoy sends the news by satellite to warning centres. Tide gauges near the coast add more clues. Today dozens of these buoys sit in the Pacific, Atlantic and Indian Oceans, and India runs its own in the Bay of Bengal and the Arabian Sea.

{{story:satellites-help}}

Iron from a blast furnace has too much carbon. In a basic oxygen furnace, pure oxygen is blown into it at high speed. The oxygen burns off extra carbon in a shower of sparks, and the iron becomes steel in about 20 minutes.

Steelmakers then add small amounts of other metals for special jobs. Chromium makes stainless steel that resists rust, used for kitchen sinks and steel tiffins. Manganese makes steel tougher for rail tracks. The liquid steel is poured into moulds or cast in long glowing strips, then squeezed between rollers into shape.

{{story:industrial-revolution}}

The conductor stands in front and shows the beat with their hands or a stick called a baton. They signal when to start, when to get louder or softer and when to stop. A tiny lift of the hand can tell sixty singers to breathe at the same moment.

Breathing matters a lot. Singers fill their [[@lungs-breathe|lungs]] deeply, using the muscle under the ribs called the [[diaphragm|diaphragm]]. Then they let the air out slowly and steadily, so long notes don't wobble. In big choirs, singers sometimes take turns to breathe during a long note, so the sound never seems to stop.

{{story:voice}}

The cliff at Ellora is made of [[basalt|basalt]], a dark, hard rock [[@earth-weird-giants-causeway|formed when lava cooled]]. About 66 million years ago, enormous volcanic eruptions poured layer after layer of [[lava|lava]] across western India, creating what [[geologist|geologists]] call the [[@deccan|Deccan Traps]].

Basalt is tough but even, so a skilled carver can cut it into sharp, fine shapes. The flat layers of old lava flows also helped workers judge levels as they cut down into the hill.

{{story:deccan}}

In 1974, farmers near Xi'an in [[@ancient-china|China]] were digging a well when they found pieces of a clay figure. Archaeologists soon uncovered pits full of life-sized soldiers, horses and chariots. They were made for the tomb of Qin Shi Huang, the first emperor of a united China, who died in 210 BCE.

Experts estimate there are about 8,000 figures, though not all have been dug up yet. Workers made bodies in moulds, then added faces, hair and armour by hand, so the soldiers look like individuals. Each carried real bronze weapons.

{{story:ancient-china}}

India passed its first cooperative law in 1904, mainly to set up credit societies so that farmers could borrow without falling into the hands of moneylenders. After independence, cooperatives spread into sugar mills, [[fertiliser|fertiliser]], housing and banks. In 1950, farmers near Loni in Maharashtra opened one of the first cooperative sugar factories in the country. Hundreds followed across the state, and the farmers who ran them became powerful local leaders. The fertiliser maker IFFCO, founded in 1967, is also owned by thousands of farmer co-ops.

The most famous is the dairy cooperative movement started in Anand, Gujarat, in 1946, which grew into [[@amul|Amul]]. Milk co-ops later spread across India during Operation Flood and helped make India the world's largest milk producer. In 2021, the Government of India set up a separate Ministry of Cooperation to look after the sector.

{{story:white-revolution}}

In 1834, Britain ended slavery in its [[colony|colonies]]. Plantation owners needed new workers, so from 1838 to 1917 more than a million Indians were taken across the oceans as indentured labourers. They signed contracts to work for years on sugar and rubber farms.

They went to Mauritius, Fiji, Trinidad and Tobago, Guyana, Suriname, South Africa and more. They took very little with them, but they carried their stories, songs and festivals. Their [[descendant|descendants]] still celebrate Diwali, which is why it is a public holiday in many of those countries.

{{story:indian-diaspora}}

In 1972, countries signed a [[treaty|treaty]] at UNESCO in Paris called the World Heritage Convention. They promised to protect special places at home and help protect them elsewhere.

The first 12 sites were listed in 1978. They included the [[@galapagos|Galápagos Islands]] and Yellowstone in the USA. India's first four came in 1983: the Taj Mahal, Agra Fort, and the cave temples of Ajanta and [[@rashtrakutas|Ellora]].

{{story:ellora-kailasa}}

The Academy was founded in 1927 by people from the [[@hollywood|Hollywood]] film industry. Two years later, on 16 May 1929, it held its first awards dinner at a hotel in Hollywood. About 270 guests attended. The winners had been announced months earlier, and the handing out took around 15 minutes.

By the 1950s the ceremony was on television. Today it is a huge live show with songs, jokes and a red carpet where photographers crowd around famous guests.

{{story:los-angeles}}

Pelé was injured early in the 1962 World Cup, but Brazil won it without him. In 1970, in Mexico, he played in one of the best teams ever. Brazil won the final 4–1 against Italy, and Pelé scored with a header.

The 1970 World Cup was one of the first shown widely on colour television, so millions saw Pelé's skills in bright yellow shirts. He scored more than 1,000 goals in his career, though many came in friendly matches.

{{story:fifa-world-cup}}

In 2011 scientists at McGill University in Canada asked people to bring their favourite pieces of music. They scanned their brains while they listened. At the best moments, the brain released dopamine, the same chemical involved in enjoying food.

The surprise was the timing. Dopamine also rose a few seconds before the big moment, as people waited for it. Many listeners got [[@music-goosebumps|goosebumps]] or a shiver down the spine right then. Your brain enjoys the build-up almost as much as the payoff.

{{story:mozart}}

On ordinary railways, track is divided into blocks, and only one train is allowed in each block at a time. Coloured signals beside the track tell drivers when a block is clear. Blocks have to be long enough for a train to stop safely inside them, so trains must stay well apart.

Many modern metros use a smarter system, often called communication-based train control. Each train constantly radios its exact position and speed to a control computer. The computer works out a safe "bubble" ahead of each train and tells it how fast it may go. If a train gets too close to the one in front, the system [[@brakes|brakes]] it automatically. Because the bubbles move with the trains, they can run closer together, safely.

{{story:urban-planning}}

The empire reached its peak under Suleiman I, who ruled from 1520 to 1566. Europeans called him "the Magnificent". In Turkey he is known as Kanuni, "the Lawgiver", because he organised the empire's laws.

His armies took Belgrade and much of Hungary, and in 1529 besieged Vienna, though they failed to take it. His navy controlled much of the Mediterranean. The architect Mimar Sinan, who served Suleiman and later sultans, designed more than 300 buildings, including the Süleymaniye Mosque, whose domes still crown one of Istanbul's hills.

{{story:coffee}}

The Central Pacific faced the hardest job straight away: the Sierra Nevada mountains, with [[granite|granite]] cliffs and snow many metres deep. Workers hung over cliffs to cut ledges and blasted [[@tunnels|tunnels]] through solid rock with black powder and later a dangerous explosive called nitroglycerin. Some tunnels advanced only about 30 cm a day.

Winters were brutal. Avalanches swept away camps, and some workers were buried in snow and not found until spring.

{{story:tunnels}}

The Colosseum opened in 80 CE. It could hold about 50,000 people, who came to watch [[@gladiators|gladiators]] and wild animal fights. [[@earthquakes|Earthquakes]] and stone robbers took parts of it away, but most of its outer walls still stand. Visitors can see the tunnels underneath, where animals and fighters waited.

The Pantheon, rebuilt around 125 CE under the emperor Hadrian, has a concrete dome about 43 m across. At the top is a round opening, the oculus, about 9 m wide. When it rains, water falls into the building and drains away through small holes in the floor. Roman concrete was made with volcanic ash, and scientists are still studying why it lasts so well.

{{story:roman-numerals}}

Long before Ctesias, people of the Indus Valley carved small stone seals, about 4,500 years ago. The most common animal on them looks like a bull with a single horn. [[archaeologist|Archaeologists]] often call it the "unicorn" seal.

Did they believe in a one-horned beast? Some think so. Others think it is simply a bull drawn side-on, with one horn hiding the other. Because nobody can read the [[@indus-script|Indus script]] yet, the real meaning is a mystery.

{{story:indus-script}}

In [[@hyderabad|Hyderabad]], kacchi biryani layers raw marinated meat with rice and cooks them together on dum. Lucknow's biryani, from the kitchens of Awadh, is gentler and more delicate, made with meat cooked first.

[[@kolkata|Kolkata]] biryani is lightly spiced and usually includes a potato and an egg. Many people link it to Wajid Ali Shah, the last Nawab of Awadh, who was exiled to Kolkata in 1856 with his cooks. Malabar biryani from Kozhikode uses short, fragrant rice called kaima. Ambur and Dindigul in Tamil Nadu make spicy versions with short-grain rice too.

{{story:mughals}}

Historians call the swap of plants and animals between the Americas and the rest of the world the Columbian Exchange. Europeans took horses, wheat and sugarcane to the Americas. Ships brought back [[@corn-maize|maize]], potatoes, tomatoes, cocoa, [[@chillies|chillies]], pineapples and peanuts.

Portuguese ships sailed regularly between Brazil, Africa and Goa, so many American plants reached India through Portuguese ports. Some were grown first in Goa and Kerala before spreading inland. Within a couple of centuries, Indians were cooking with chillies and snacking on guavas as if they had always been here.

{{story:columbus}}
